<section class="panel" aria-label="2021 first-party mathematical meaning of subtraction algorithms part 2">
  <h2>Warm-up: True or False? Does It Commute?</h2>
  <p>Discuss the three statements: <strong>4 × 5 = 5 × 4</strong>; <strong>125 + 200 = 200 + 125</strong>; <strong>300 − 100 = 100 − 300</strong>. Explain why subtraction behaves differently.</p>
  <h2>Activity 1: Revise Subtraction Work</h2>
  <p>Lin's original illustrated work for 428 − 156 contains the error of reversing the two tens-values instead of exchanging place-value units. Work with a partner and first explain why 20 − 50 does not equal 50 − 20.</p>
  <div role="img" aria-label="First-party independent place-value correction of 428 minus 156, answer 272" style="border:1px solid #8daeb7;border-radius:8px;padding:12px">
    <h3>How to give 20 five more tens to subtract?</h3>
    <p>428 = <strong>400 + 20 + 8</strong> = <strong>300 + 120 + 8</strong> (one hundred becomes ten tens).</p>
    <p>Now remove 100 + 50 + 6. Left: <strong>200 + 70 + 2 = 272</strong>.</p><p>Proof: 272 + 156 = 428.</p>
    <p>This is independently authored text, not the original Lin illustration.</p>
  </div>
  <h2>Activity 2: Try the Algorithm</h2>
  <p>Use an expanded-form subtraction method. Under the original accessibility option, choose at least three of five; record and explain the exchanged tens/hundreds.</p>
  <ol><li>283 − 159</li><li>425 − 192</li><li>639 − 465</li><li>591 − 128</li><li>832 − 575</li></ol>
  <h2>Teacher-reviewed explanation and synthesis</h2>
  <p>Describe Lin's precise mistake and revised steps, using <em>decompose, hundreds, tens, ones</em>. Explain why subtraction is not commutative. MLR8 discussion or concrete base-ten blocks may be used.</p>
